ein we have very high antibody  diversity  usually between 500 and 5000 clusters or  clonotypes depending on the target and  the salt concentration  um and as i mentioned the fast cov2  spike protein selections  yield antibodies that are better than  most immune antibodies  generated from convalescent patients and  within twofold of the very best  antibodies that have been described to  date  we've also found that uh if you do next  gen sequencing  and uh cluster then you can increase  your cluster diversity by up to 50 fold  and the the the amount that you increase  depends on how many  uh sanger clones you you sequence at the  beginning  and so just to turn quickly to the  people that did the work  and so the libraries were constructed by  andrei texera in collaboration with  frank erasmus who did a lot of  bioinformatic work to define the cdrs  and how we were building them sarah  d'angelo built the  generation 2 libraries and as the cto of  specifica  and uh keeps the whole lab running  unfortunately and camilla  are doing the selections this work was  done in collaboration with  rutgers in particular the lab of a  pinter  who did some of the pseudo-violent  neutralization assays  and also with scripps um dennis burton  and dele huang  who did the live virus neutralizations  and with that i'd like to thank you  if you can visit us at specifically.bio  send me an email  a bradbury at specific bio if you have  any further questions  um we supply libraries we do selections  we're  happy to help you with your antibody  discovery  campaigns and so with that i'll stop and  i'll pass over to  noah  and thank you andrea and noah for that  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have any questions you'd like to  ask please do so now just click on that  ask a question box located on the far  left of your screen  and we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for  so let's get started it looks like we  already have some great questions  coming in from our audience  our first question is  how many antibodies libraries have you  made  can you hear me yes uh so as i mentioned  during during my presentation  um we've made we've made up just under  70 different libraries  and the reason we make so many libraries  is is first because we've got  we can do it quite straightforwardly now  but also  we've found that it you you can't really  test a new vector for example  or a new a new selection strategy  unless you actually make a library and  do and go through the whole process we  find that if you  just make an antibody or two and then  and then do  do the work uh it gives you a full sense  of security  or or pessimism so  in order to study the different elements  when we make new things we always we  always make a new library and then test  that  thank you so much now how long does it  take  to make an antibody library  so again um i think i mentioned that  during the talk  and to make a library usually takes  about a couple of months  and when i say to make a library in that  that particular case we will actually  make five sub libraries to to  generate a whole platform and um the the  library  uh after we've made it then takes  another couple of months to do the qc  which involves  next-gen sequencing and also  um selection against the panel of  targets that we know about  and measuring the affinities looking at  the diversity of the outputs and so on  so we're we're confident uh with each  library we know we know what the parents  are  it's interesting we've now discovered  that there are next-gen sequencing  there are diversity measurements that if  we don't hit  we know the library is not going to work  very well  thank you so much and again i want to  thank our audience for these great  questions coming in our next question  can you customize antibody libraries and  if so  how does one do that so  we can do that actually a number of  different levels and we've done that  uh at a number of different levels so  for example  we've had some partners that have come  to us with particular  scaffolds that they like they say that  they've worked very well in their hands  so can we make a library in their  scaffolds we certainly we can do that  other people say can you  can you eliminate additional liabilities  and we can do that too  uh we've recently had a partner that  came and  wanted a particular liability which in  the literature is indicated as being  extremely rare but this was something  that they were they were worried about  we can also put back some of the  liabilities so we've  we've been very broad in our definition  of a liability  so if you look at the literature there's  lots of liabilities we've identified  some  from in-house work and as as we make new  oligo sets we we eliminate more and more  liabilities  but some people uh don't like don't  a bit scared about eliminating so many  liabilities and so  ask us to put some of them back so we  can we can customize at that level as  well  um and then the format single chain fab  um  the vector we've we can use our vector  we can use somebody else's vector  um we make phase display libraries we  can also make  thank you so much now we have time for a  couple more questions  are the affinities routinely as good as  you  show yeah  actually they they are and that's um  that's a good question we've had  we've had problems with our generation  one library if we got a single-digit  nanomolar affinity  we were overjoyed and so when we saw  these affinities  we were rather surprised we we were  planning to get the cartera  um we we actually got it we tested some  antibodies on it and we were sure that  we were using it  incorrectly because the affinities were  were so high  so we actually sent the antibodies that  we were testing to kartera  and they they tested them for us and and  confirmed  that the confirmed that the the  affinities were what we say so  yeah we as in general um it's sometimes  target specific peptides for example  tend to be  much less uh much less good but on the  whole  it's 20 sub nanomolar and 40 between 1  and 10  on the whole thank you very much  we have time for one  more question do you have any plans to  market your stars kovi to  antibodies well  we're in a glut there's so many other  antibodies out there we'd love to market  them if anybody's listening is  interested then please get in touch  yeah andrew and noah thank you so much  for your time today would you like to  provide any  closing remarks for our audience members  before we go  sure um i i see there's a couple of more  questions i can i can just address those  quickly why do you think there's a poor  correlation between ic50 and affinity  when comparing rbd and trimer i just  don't know i think it's something to do  with orientation with epitopes being  hidden but we're not the only ones to  find that i was speaking to  johanna hanson at regeneron recently and  she said exactly the same thing  it's the difference between biology and  biophysics i think  um does the starting pool of clinical  antibodies change depending on the  target  uh we basically whatever target  we do we get lots and lots of different  uh binders as i've talked about  so and and just in closing um we're very  very collaborative very happy to work  with anybody that's interested in  in in accessing our platform and uh go  to our website you see all the details  there  i'd be happy to talk to you further  thank you so much andrew and noah would  you like to  provide any closing remarks definitely  i'd just  like to reiterate our thanks to andrew  for providing a great talk  and really getting some great insight  into how specifica  does things and really the unique ways  in that they do them  and to thank our audience in general  we're really excited to talk about the  lsa today and how it's  supported specifically in these efforts  and  please anybody has any questions  additional questions that we couldn't  get to today please just reach out  and we'll follow up with those as soon  as we can thank you  and thank you gentlemen both of you  andrew and noah for your time today and  for your important research  i also want to thank lab roots and our  sponsor cartera for underwriting today's  educational webcast  audience before we go i want to thank  you for your interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time  of registration and last of all this  webcast can  be viewed on demand lab routes will  alert you via email  when it's available for replay we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  until next time  stay healthy and take care everyone bye  bye for now
 

